<h3>The operating loop — how the team works</h3>
<p>Once the team is made, the orchestrator turns the loop below. The core is <strong>"split it up and go in parallel, verify with tests, and if it is wrong do it again"</strong>.</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Decompose</strong> — split the goal into work units. <em>e.g. impact analysis · hold logic · notification · regression tests</em></li>
    <li><strong>Assign &amp; run in parallel</strong> — each agent handles its assigned task at the same time.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify</strong> — QA cross-checks the results (standard feature regression, numerical consistency).</li>
    <li><strong>Feedback loop</strong> — if there is a problem, it goes back to the agent in charge to be redone. Repeat until it passes.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Agent teams vs isolated subagents</h3>
<p>Claude Code's default <strong>subagents</strong> are <strong>isolated</strong> from each other and cannot talk. An <strong>agent team</strong> does <strong>communicate</strong> — for example, if the development agent finds that "the standard source information is insufficient", it can <strong>wake the analysis agent back up</strong> and have it investigate further.</p>

